tasks/BACKLOG.md:1603:🔬 **F-2072-1 (s2072 2026-08-20, TENTH DRY BOARD — THE OLDEST UNTOUCHED DESK PREMISE IS NOT OWNER-GATED AND HAS NOT BEEN FOR ELEVEN DAYS: F-E2S-3's FORK WAS RULED 2026-08-09, ITS RULED HALF IS MERGED AND LIVE, AND ITS SURVIVING HALF'S TECHNICAL PREMISE IS REFUTED BY A RATIFIED SPEC).** 📋 **THE SELECTION.** s2071's (B) named **F-E2S-3** as one of the two oldest untouched desk premises and sharpened the rule: *a premise stated in a component's VERB should be checked for whether that component had any alternative*. F-E2S-3 states its premise as an asserted ABSENCE — *"the headless sim has no pressure-to-damage consumer"*, *"the board sells no weapon that can touch the railcar"* — and an asserted absence is the cheapest kind to check, because it names the thing to go look for. 🎯 **THE ROW'S GATE READS `GATE: owner word on the fork`, AND THE OWNER GAVE THAT WORD ELEVEN DAYS AGO.** `tasks/BACKLOG.md:335` — **OWNER RULINGS ROUNDUP (2026-08-09 evening, desk walkthrough)** — carries as answer **2** the words *"F-E2S-3 RULED: de-list now, socket later"*: fork **(c)** now, fork **(a)** later, fork (b) refused. **The row at `:505` never learned it.** 📊 **ALL THREE FORKS ARE RESOLVED, each verified this fire by a command rather than by the ledger's claim. (c) RULED, MERGED, AND STILL LIVE:** `88530e3ef` (2026-08-09T23:37) de-listed the three contracts, and `git merge-base --is-ancestor 88530e3ef main` confirms it. ⚠️ **It was then SILENTLY REVERSED AND REPAIRED, which is exactly why the CODE had to be read and the merge could not be trusted:** ap16-4 turned the door from a list into a derivation (*"12 contracts → 22"*), re-admitting all three, and `f1660-1-door-readmission-repair` (leaf `merged`) restored the refusal as **cited exemptions**. ✓ **VERIFIED IN TODAY'S TREE:** `CONTRACT_ADMISSION_EXEMPTIONS` carries `e2-hill-mine`, `e2-incline` and `e2-trestle`, each with `citation: 'F-E2S-3'`, and `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` filters them out of the derived door. **(b) never taken, moot. (a) REFUTED IN ITS PREMISE.** 🔑 **THE REFUTATION, and it is the half worth reading: fork (a) asks to "socket the pressure-to-damage consumer headless", and THERE IS NOTHING TO SOCKET — the consumer is not missing.** `specs/epoch-saga/e2-railcar-pressure-socket.md` (**Status: RATIFIED 2026-08-13**) measured this, and I re-measured every load-bearing claim against today's code rather than inheriting it: the railcar is an ordinary enemy (`EnemyEliteKind = 'baron' | 'railcar'`, `Enemy.ts:17`) taking **ordinary projectile damage** through the single generic `Enemy.takeDamage(amount)` (`:774`), reached from `CombatSystem.ts:727` and `:758`; and **`CombatSystem.ts` returns ZERO matches for `immune`/`invulnerab`/`railcar`**, so there is no special case and no immunity anywhere on that path. **The railcar already bleeds to normal hits.** ➡️ **THE ACTUAL CAUSE IS AN UNLOCK GATE, NOT AN ABSENT SOCKET.** The three pressure weapons (`boilerLance`/`pressureMortar`/`skyRocket`, `PressureArsenalSystem.ts:7`) register as ordinary `combat.registerShooter` clients — real damage, pressure-costed — but `PressureArsenalSystem` is CONSTRUCTED behind `hasResearchNode(...)`, **`hasBaronMedal()`** and an epoch predicate (`Game.ts:1372-1381`; the spec's `1353-1358` has **rotted ~19 lines** in seven days — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts). A headless run holds no medal, so the arsenal never activates and nothing it owns ever fires. **The owner confirmed the mechanism from his own play, verbatim 2026-08-13: *"no, I did not have the weapons then"*.** 🪦 **AND THE WORK THAT FOLLOWED IS ALREADY SPENT — TWO MASTERS AUTHORED, BOTH REJECTED ON MERIT, BOTH NOW `superseded`, SO NOTHING HERE IS QUEUEABLE EITHER.** `e2-railcar-arsenal-floor` (v1) — `gate-side` HOLD s1741, F-1741-1..4: it *fabricated pressure only in HeadlessContractSim, granted `boiler_battery` through a broad research bypass, and multiplied the three weapons' damage by 6 and range to 20*. `e2-railcar-arsenal-floor-v2` — `gate-side` HOLD s1755, and it **STOPPED HONESTLY at the measured economy gap** exactly as its master ordered: hero dies **wave 7** against a **wave-12** railcar; one boiler yields **120 pressure/wave** against a **~305** full-arsenal cost; the railcar's **~3,489 HP took 0 damage**. Both were retired in s1856 bookkeeping, v2's reason verbatim: *"the owner's continuous-campaign direction makes this cold-start benchmark question moot. Do not re-queue v2 as-is; E2 now needs a separately scoped Layer-2 pressure-arsenal socket using campaign-carried progression."* ⚖️ **WHAT IS ACTUALLY OWED, AND BY WHOM: AN ATTENDED SPEC, NOT AN OWNER WORD.** The surviving need is the Layer-2 socket named above; §2E forbids a fire authoring where no spec slice exists, and scoping is attended work. **This is the F-2063-2 class — attended-owed work sitting in an owner-desk slot.** 🚫 **WHAT I DO NOT CLAIM, because "the fork is discharged" is not "no owner content exists":** the ratified spec carries **two live one-word hooks**, both §7.4 veto windows rather than gates — the arsenal-floor **design ruling** (*"a contract that fields the railcar boss must itself provide the means to beat it … Reverse with one word if the gate was meant to be hard"*) and **Q2 damage-feel** (which of the three weapons reads as the railcar-cracker; attended default **skyRocket**, *"still open, non-blocking"*, settled in a balance slice). **The v2 supersession may have mooted the first of those as well — campaign-carried progression replaces an arsenal floor — but that is an INFERENCE and I did not measure it.** I also did **not** re-run either master's evidence: the wave-7/wave-12, 120-vs-305 and 3,489 HP figures are **quoted from the leaf, not re-measured**. I did **not** flip the row's head marker, deliberately — **the defect is still live** (the three maps remain de-listed and unwinnable headless), and only its GATE changed. ⓘ **One date disagrees and the ledger wins:** the spec says the fork was ruled *2026-08-06*, while the roundup header at `:335` and the merge commit's own message both say **2026-08-09** — two independent sources against one. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF: A LOCKED CAPABILITY AND AN ABSENT CAPABILITY PRODUCE IDENTICAL SYMPTOMS AND CALL FOR OPPOSITE CURES.** One is a gate to open, the other a system to build, and the gap between them here was a whole census-stream slice nobody needed. F-E2S-3 observed *"no weapon touches the railcar"* — which was TRUE — and inferred *"the board sells no such weapon"*, when the board sells three and a medal check keeps them holstered. **This is the third consecutive dry-fire finding of one shape:** s2070's *uncalibrated vs drifted*, s2071's *a chooser vs a missing candidate*, and now *locked vs absent*. **In every one, the observation was sound and the NOUN in the diagnosis was wrong — so re-measure the diagnosis, not the observation.** **REC: one owner word retires F-E2S-3 from the desk; the residual belongs in an attended spec.** **Non-blocking. GATE: RE-CLASSIFIED — no owner word is owed on the fork; the Layer-2 socket needs an attended spec before any master can be authored.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1614:✅ **F-2063-2 — [DISCHARGED s2102, see F-2102-2 in this row] AN ATTENDED WAIT-LOOP HAD BEEN POLLING FOR 3d15h ON A CONDITION ONLY AN ATTENDED SESSION CAN SATISFY, AND A PLANNED WORK BATCH IS PARKED BEHIND IT (s2063; OWNER/ATTENDED — one command to clear, but not a fire's to run).** 📏 **WHAT WAS MEASURED, from the process table and the goal tree, not inferred.** `pid 59013` (`PPID 40448` = the attended `claude --resume` on `ttys035`; `TTY ??`, `Ss`, started **Sat Aug 15 21:05:26**, elapsed **03-15:09:25**, CPU **0:02.39**) runs an `until … ; do sleep 120; done` loop whose predicate is that **both** `assay-replay-fidelity` **and** `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` read `status:"merged"` in `tasks/goals.json`. On success it echoes its purpose verbatim: *"BOTH DRAINED at $(date +%H:%M) — **queue worker-loop (lane-d) + season-roll/E6 (main), land pending batch**"*. 📊 **THE PREDICATE IS HALF-SATISFIED AND THE OTHER HALF CANNOT SELF-CLEAR.** `assay-replay-fidelity` → **`merged`** at `3a4a5d15` (2026-08-15, hours after the loop started). `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` → **`blocked`**, and `drain-block-check` prints its class: *"s1815 **GATE-SIDE HOLD, NOT MERGED** … Cure with an **attended** assertion/public-surface re-scope and detached re-land; E6 stays held."* ⚠️ **SO THE LOOP IS WAITING ON ITS OWN OWNER.** The blocker's stated cure requires an attended session; the attended session is the thing blocked, sleeping in 120-second increments. **No fire can lift it** — §3.0's STOP is total for a blocked leaf, and a `gate-side` hold is explicitly *not* something a green battery clears. Left alone, this loop polls forever: **2,630 wake-ups so far**, and nothing about the next 2,630 differs. 🔗 **IT IS NOT AN ISOLATED SLEEPING SHELL — IT IS THE VISIBLE END OF A TWO-LEAF CHAIN THAT IS THE WHOLE DRY BOARD.** The board's **only** two blocked leaves are this one and `assay-season-roll`, and the second is blocked *by* the first: *"E5 admission completion has NOT happened — `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` is itself a gate-side hold (F-1815-2) whose drain rider was to spawn the admission-completion successor, so that successor was never spawned"* (master parked at `tasks/queue-paused/assay-season-roll.md`). The batch the loop means to land — **lane-d worker-loop + main season-roll/E6** — is exactly the work `authorable-candidates` cannot offer, on a board reading **13 planned / 0 fire-authorable** with **all four lanes USABLE and idle**. 💰 **COST, SIZED HONESTLY AND NOT INFLATED: the compute is nothing** — 2.39 seconds of CPU across 3½ days, a `sleep` loop by construction. **The cost is the belief.** A monitor that will announce *"BOTH DRAINED"* reads, to whoever set it, like work that resumes by itself; while it sleeps, four idle lanes and a paused main master wait on an event that has no producer. That is Mistake #5's shape (a clock running long past the thing it measures) crossed with Mistake #4 (a premise true when written — the leaf genuinely *was* mid-drain on 08-15 — and never re-checked). ⓘ **AND IT IS INVISIBLE FROM INSIDE.** An attended session cannot see its own child sleeping, and no fire duty looks at the process table for waiters; this was found only because a dry board left room to ask why the factory was quiet. ➡️ **THE ACT IS THE OWNER'S / ATTENDED'S, AND IT IS SMALL: either (a) do the E5 admission re-scope the hold names — attended assertion/public-surface re-scope + detached re-land from `save/fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket-s1815-admission-hold` (`4429ea26`) — which unblocks BOTH leaves and lets the loop fire honestly, or (b) `kill 59013` and land the batch by hand.** A fire may do neither: (a) is attended by the blocker's own words, and (b) is killing a live attended process, which §7.7 and the never-kill-by-an-old-pid rule both forbid. 🚫 **NO MECHANISM IS PROPOSED, DELIBERATELY.** The obvious one — have some guard scan for stale `until`-loops — would fire on every legitimate monitor a session sets during a normal drain wait, and would be excused into uselessness inside a week (the `cross-engine` label's fate, F-1460-1). **GATE: closes when `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` leaves `blocked`, or when the owner rules on the parked batch.** ✅ **F-2102-2 — DISCHARGED, AND NOT BY ANYONE ACTING ON THIS ROW: THE BLOCKER CLEARED ITSELF, THE BATCH LANDED WITHIN THE HOUR, AND THE LOOP EXITED — ~17h50m BEFORE I READ IT (measured s2102, 2026-08-21).** 📏 **THE GATE'S FIRST DISJUNCT, SETTLED BY ONE COMMAND:** `node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` → **`✅ CLEAR … status="merged"`**. So did its partner: `assay-replay-fidelity` → **`merged`**. **Both halves of the loop's predicate are satisfied, so the loop's own exit condition fired.** 🕰️ **THE TIMELINE, EVERY STEP FROM `git log`/`goals.json`/`ps` RATHER THAN FROM THIS ROW:** `08-15 21:05:26` loop starts (pid 59013) · `08-15 21:38:58` `assay-replay-fidelity` → `merged` (**33 minutes in — the loop spent its entire remaining life waiting on ONE leaf, not two**) · `08-18 20:30` s2056 parks `assay-season-roll` · `08-19` **this row is filed** · `08-20 08:27:39` the dredge-queen fix merges (`26a364bf`) · **`08-20 08:54:28` the leaf flips `merged` (`a1e8bc63c`) — THE GATE IS MET HERE** · `08-20 09:23:28` **the parked batch itself lands** (`23c3ea98`, *"season roll — county board rolls to assayed season 2"*) · `08-20 15:40:44` s2086 retires the spent master and **`tasks/queue-paused/` goes empty** · `08-21 02:44` me. 🔍 **VERIFIED AT THE PROCESS TABLE, NOT ASSUMED FROM THE LEDGER — the two can disagree and only one of them is the actual subject of this finding:** **pid 59013 is GONE**, and a filtered sweep of the whole table finds **0 surviving poll loops**. `drain-block-check --all` reports **561 leaves scanned, 2 BLOCKED**, and **neither is this chain** (`b4-picnic-three-stake-hold` superseded · `e10-lore-owner-read` owner-read). `tasks/queue-paused/` is empty. **Nothing is parked, nothing is blocked, nothing is polling.** ⚠️ **BUT NOTE WHAT THAT SWEEP ALMOST TOLD ME, BECAUSE IT IS F-2102-1's DEFECT RECURRING INSIDE THE SAME FIRE, ONE HOUR LATER:** my first `ps` filter for surviving loops matched a process immediately — **my own `claude -p` fire**, whose argv contains the entire fire protocol and therefore *quotes* every phrase I was grepping for. **A process that merely NARRATES the hazard is not an instance of it, exactly as a log line that narrates `FIRE END rc=1` is not a fire ending.** Excluding the quoters (`claude -p` / `--resume` / `FIRE PROTOCOL`) drops the count to zero. *The same contamination class caught me twice in one fire, on two unrelated instruments, in two different corpora — argv and log text. **Assume any corpus that stores agent output contains every string you plan to search it for.*** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it is the sharper version of what F-2102-1 found one row over: this row's cost was never the compute — the row said so itself ("2.39 seconds of CPU across 3½ days … the cost is the belief").** What it could not anticipate is that **the belief outlived the condition**: the chain self-cleared on 08-20 and the row went on asserting a parked batch and a sleeping shell for another **~17 hours and ~16 fires**, on a desk 33 deep, while every fire in that window read a dry board and reported **0 fire-authorable** — the exact scarcity this row diagnosed, still being cited after its cause was gone. **A finding whose gate a command settles decays into a false report of the board the moment it is met.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, THE PRACTICE (no mechanism — see below): re-measure a desk item's gate before carrying it, and do it for the CHEAP ones first.** Of the 33 carried items, exactly **two** had a first disjunct a single command settles — this one and [F-2062-1] — and **both were already MET**. That is a 2-for-2 hit rate on the cheapest possible check. 🚫 **NO GUARD, DELIBERATELY, AND THE PRECEDENT IS EXPLICIT:** [F-1609-1]'s walk over `GATE:` prose found **33** open-headed rows and exactly **ONE** real defect, and [F-1610-2] — while correcting a stale ladder in this same way — ruled the re-scan *"cheap, no mechanism … it must judge each hit against the row's own thread before touching a marker, exactly as I did here."* A guard over free-prose gate conditions reds on legitimately-open rows and is excused into uselessness within a week (the `cross-engine` fate, [F-1460-1]). ⓘ **AND THE SUBJECT-ZONE RULE EARNED ITS KEEP ON THIS VERY WALK:** my first pass flagged **F-1510-1** and **F-1601-1** as desk items whose own gates read *"none — closes on filing"* and *"none owed — a pointer, not a debt"*. **Both were FALSE — the rows I matched were F-2077-1 and F-1610-2 CITING them**, and a citing row states nothing about the cited id. *A grep would have condemned two innocent items; reading the subject zone exonerated them.* **GATE: none owed to the owner — closed on measurement by the row's own first disjunct; the batch it guarded shipped at `23c3ea98`. Reversible with one word.** Related: [F-2102-1], [F-2062-1], [F-2056-1], [F-2086-1], [F-1609-1], [F-1610-2].
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1629:🟠 **F-2056-1 — THE STALL HAD A SECOND UNDISPATCHED CASUALTY, AND ITS ONLY GATE LIVED IN BACKLOG PROSE THAT NO DECISIVE INSTRUMENT READS (s2056; caught by self-audit before the runner could consume it).** 📏 **WHAT WAS MEASURED.** s2055 cured the dead implementer (F-2055-1) and dispatched the one master it found sitting in a queue. Sweeping `ls -t tasks/*.md` by MTIME rather than by leaf status found a second one from the same 2026-08-15 evening authoring batch: `tasks/assay-season-roll.md` (authored 20:19, between `assay-replay-fidelity` 20:12 and `assay-worker-loop` 20:23), never `cp`'d into any queue, undispatched for 3 days. It implements a VERBATIM owner ruling (`specs/agent-play/tape-contract.md:18-20`, RATIFIED 2026-08-15: *"this kind of calls for a next season? Because we have to test the taping anyways again."*), its stated dependency `assay-cf-lifecycle` is merged (`49aab8f1d63a2175e47e85b54659fead6caaa6da`, 39 `assay` hits live in `functions/api/standings.ts`), and it is NOT stale (Mistake #8 clear): `boardKey()` at `functions/api/standings.ts:887` is `standings:${epochId}:${contractId}` with NO season dimension, so scope item 1 is genuinely unbuilt. The 6 `season` hits in that file are `resolveSeasonAt()` DISPLAY labels — precisely the false positive the master's own no-op guard anticipates. ⚠️ **THE DEFECT: I DISPATCHED IT, AND EVERY DECISIVE INSTRUMENT SAID GO.** `drain-block-check` → **CLEAR** (`status="planned"`, no block). The leaf's own note → **"MAIN slot; queue when main frees"**, and main WAS free. Spec ratified, dependency merged, not stale, main queue empty, MAIN THROTTLE clear (0 undrained done-moves). The master file itself carries **no** `QUEUE ONLY AFTER` line, no `GATE:` line, no sequencing clause anywhere — `grep -n "QUEUE ONLY AFTER|after E5|admission|GATE"` matches ONE line, its unrelated `READY-FOR-GATES` sign-off. The gate exists in exactly one place on the whole board: the attended authoring-batch row in this file (find it by CONTENT — `grep -F "SEASON ROLL RULED + SLICE-2 AUTHORED + E5 WON"`; it sat at line 13 when this finding was drafted and rotted to 15 the moment the finding was prepended above it, which is the hazard in miniature), which reads *"master tasks/assay-season-roll.md (**MAIN, after E5 admission completion**)"*. That condition is UNMET and has been since it was written — `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` is itself a `gate-side` hold (F-1815-2) whose drain rider was to spawn the admission-completion successor, so the successor was never spawned. 🎯 **THE REUSABLE HALF — `PROSE-ONLY` IS NOT A BENIGN BUCKET, IT IS THE TOOL DECLINING TO ANSWER.** `authorable-candidates.mjs` was run FIRST, as the refill law requires, and reported `14 planned leaves — 0 fire-authorable (owner-gated 7, attended-owed 3, **prose-only 3**, needs-spec 1)`. Re-run against the pre-edit goals via `--root` to settle it honestly: it classified this leaf **`PROSE-ONLY assay-season-roll … (prose only, key "note" — class unreadable)`** and passed the misleading note through verbatim. It did not clear the master and it did not flag the E5 gate — **it said it could not tell.** A fire reading the summary line sees `0 fire-authorable` and treats every non-zero bucket as gated; but `owner-gated`/`attended-owed`/`needs-spec` are ANSWERS and `prose-only` is an OPEN QUESTION, and they are printed side by side as if they were the same kind of thing. **The one bucket that means "unknown" is the one where an unmet gate can hide.** 🔑 **AND THE LEAF ACTIVELY CONTRADICTED THE LEDGER:** its note says *"queue when main frees"* — a dispatch INVITATION — while the authoring-batch row states a hard predecessor. Two ledger surfaces, written by the same attended batch, disagreeing about whether the same master may run; the instrument reads the permissive one because that is the one attached to the leaf. This is the ledger-vs-name class (F-1669-1) with the surfaces swapped: there the fossil was a filename and the ledger was right; here BOTH are ledger, and the more authoritative one is the one no tool parses. 💰 **COST: ZERO, BY LUCK AND NOT BY DESIGN — WHICH IS THE PART TO WEIGH.** The dispatch sat in `tasks/queue/main/` for ~4 minutes and the runner never saw it, because §1's lock semaphore gates the MAIN slot while a fire's line-1 says `ACTIVE` — I was holding my own lock. **Against a LANE slot there is no such protection: s2055 measured lane-d claiming a queued master in ONE SECOND.** The identical mistake made against lane-a/b/c would have burned a full Codex run on work whose predecessor does not exist. ✅ **CURED THIS FIRE, REVERSIBLY:** master moved to `tasks/queue-paused/assay-season-roll.md` (parked, not deleted — RETENTION LAW), leaf re-registered `blocked` + `blockClass: "gate-side"` with the BACKLOG citation and the lift condition in its `blockedReason`, so `drain-block-check assay-season-roll.md` now exits 1 and PRINTS the gate together with the authoring-batch row as its BACKLOG context line (the tool resolves that line number itself at run time, so it never goes stale). The next fire that finds this master by mtime gets a STOP with the reason, which is the gap I fell into, closed. 🚫 **NO NEW MECHANISM PROPOSED, DELIBERATELY.** The obvious cure — teach `authorable-candidates` to parse sequencing prose out of BACKLOG rows — is a natural-language judgement, and per the standing F-1460-1 prohibition a guard that must judge prose gets excused into uselessness. The cheap, real cure is the one already performed: **when the pricing tool says `PROSE-ONLY`, that leaf is UNPRICED, not cleared — open the row and read it before dispatching, and if you dispatch anyway, record the gate you found as a `blockClass` so the question is answered mechanically ONCE instead of re-derived by every fire.** ➡️ **ATTENDED-OWED, NOT OWNER-OWED:** this unblocks when E5 admission completes (the re-scope + detached re-land already carried as s2055 NEXT (G)) — the same act unblocks BOTH `fix-e5-dredge-queen-headless-socket` and this. **Two launch-relevant masters now sit behind one attended act.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1633:🟢 **F-2055-1 CURED s2055 — THE 191-FIRE NO-OP LOOP HAD AN EXIT REACHABLE FROM INSIDE THE WHOLE TIME: THE IMPLEMENTER PROCESS WAS DEAD, AND NO TRIAGE STEP EVER ASKS.** 📏 **WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, measured before touching anything:** `pgrep -fl lane-runner-v3.sh` → **rc=1, no process**; `logs/runner-headless.log`'s last dispatch is **2026-08-15 20:13**; `tasks/.runner.lock` sat held since **Aug 15 21:41** with no owner. Meanwhile `tasks/queue/lane-d/assay-worker-loop.md` had been correctly `cp`'d into the queue at **Aug 15 22:50** and never consumed. **The factory had no implementer for ~3 days and 191 consecutive fires reported a "dry board" instead.** ✓ **THE QUEUED MASTER WAS LAWFUL ON EVERY AXIS I COULD CHECK, which is what makes the stall expensive rather than merely untidy** — `drain-block-check` → **CLEAR** (not blocked, no owner fork); its own stated gate is *"QUEUE ONLY AFTER assay-replay-fidelity MERGES"* and that predecessor's leaf reads **`status:"merged"`**; its spec `specs/agent-play/assay-worker.md` is **RATIFIED 2026-08-15** carrying the owner's build GO verbatim (*"you can buld the five slices"*) **and an owner LAUNCH-GATING ruling** (*"the gauntlet verification worker queue has to be done before launch - people will play and submit results"*); the master itself is tracked and committed (`d240c669f`); lane-d was `ahead=0` **USABLE**; and **none of its three deliverables exist on main** (`scripts/assay-worker.mjs`, `scripts/assay-worker.test.mjs`, `docs/assay-worker-runbook.md` all absent), so this is not Mistake #8 stale-master shape. An owner-ruled launch blocker sat one dead process away from being built. ⚠️ **THE STRUCTURAL DEFECT IS IN §2's TRIAGE LADDER, NOT IN INSTRUMENTATION — AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE FINDING.** The instrument exists, is prescribed, and was reporting it: `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status` prints **`runner : ALIVE`/dead as its FIRST LINE**, and `CLAUDE.md` §1 lists it as a session-start read ("the li